WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.968
-Our next guest is a very
talented actress and comedian

00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:05.467
you know from shows like
"After Life" and "Motherland."

00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:07.634
Her very funny show
"Cunk on Earth"

00:00:07.634 --> 00:00:10.501
is streaming now on Netflix.
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.501 --> 00:00:13.167
-Just look at this 14th-century
painting of Jesus

00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:15.300
having his fateful tea party.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.400
The dimensions are all wrong.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.067
The room's squashed up

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:22.234
so it looks like it's happening
in the lid of a cardboard box.

00:00:22.234 --> 00:00:25.167
Jesus is twice as big
as the others.

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:27.167
So it looks like it's enjoying
an intimate dinner

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:29.701
with some schoolboys --
not a good look.

00:00:29.701 --> 00:00:32.100
And what are those,
bowls or seashells?

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.067
It's just shoddy craftsmanship.
Absolutely disgraceful.

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:37.801
Shouldn't even be in a museum.

00:00:37.801 --> 00:00:40.367
But da Vinci's reboot
of the same painting

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:41.767
is a different story.

00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:44.200
He knew how to perspective
the [Bleep] out of things.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.801
Look at the angles of the walls
in "The Last Supper"

00:00:46.801 --> 00:00:49.300
and the table there.
You almost feel like

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.000
you could crawl inside it
and betray Jesus yourself.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.300
[ Laughter ]

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.767
-Please welcome to the show
Diane Morgan, everybody.

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:58.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:01.634
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:07.167
♪♪

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:12.701
♪♪

00:01:12.701 --> 00:01:14.400
Welcome to the show, Diane.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.367
-Well, thanks for having me.

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:18.200
-I'm really thrilled to
have you.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.601
I keep saying everybody
has been talking about "Cunk"

00:01:20.601 --> 00:01:23.167
for the last month to me.
And I love --

00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:24.501
I mean, the name Philomena Cunk

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:26.133
is such a wonderful
character name.

00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:27.534
-Yeah.
-When you first saw it,

00:01:27.534 --> 00:01:29.634
did you know,
"Oh, that's really good"?

00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:31.667
-Uh, yeah.
I thought, "That's weird."

00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:34.534
-Yeah.
-And, well, it turns out

00:01:34.534 --> 00:01:36.901
that they wanted,
like, a posh character.

00:01:36.901 --> 00:01:39.367
And I'm not posh.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:43.634
And so, I went into the audition
for Philomena,

00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:47.033
did my best posh.
And but I knew

00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:49.234
that it would be just better
in my own accent,

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:51.501
because it's so flat.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.501 --> 00:01:54.100
And depressing.
It just adds something.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.868
-Well, it is -- The most
wonderful thing is,

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:59.234
you have this character who
is telling us about history

00:01:59.234 --> 00:02:03.334
and just seems not only
disinterested, deeply bored

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:04.968
by everything.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:07.200
And I think that's where the
flatness really comes in handy.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.634
-Absolutely.
'Cause I know

00:02:08.634 --> 00:02:11.100
that everyone watching it
is bored by history, as well.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.234
[ Laughter ]
So I know what people want.

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:16.267
-Yeah.
-I know.

00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:19.000
I know, when someone is being
really boring and dull,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.000
to move it on.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.300
Or just yawn in their face.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.300
-It's true because when
you watch those history shows

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.868
where the host is
really excited about it,

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:29.000
it makes you feel bad
because you don't share it.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.934
-Yeah, 'cause you're thinking,
"This is boring."

00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:33.434
-Do you have any personal
interest in history yourself?

00:02:33.434 --> 00:02:35.434
-No, I hate it.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:39.100
Boring. Unless it's
something really weird.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.300
-Okay. So you --
-I like weird stuff.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.901
-Yeah.
-I like to hear

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:45.200
about the Bermuda Triangle,
stuff like that.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.701
-Okay, gotcha. So, that's --

00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:49.200
-I realize that's not history.
But, you know...

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.300
I like -- Just saying
I like weird stuff.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.367
If it's not weird,
I'm not interested.

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:58.000
-Yeah. So, you -- I know you've
done a couple series of this.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.100
You did "Cunk in Britain,"
and this is "Cunk on Earth."

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.167
One of the things
that's been a through line is

00:03:03.167 --> 00:03:05.667
you do talk
to very informed people,

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:08.501
experts, professors.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.501 --> 00:03:12.300
And I mean, it's so wonderful
to watch you talk to them,

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.634
because you ask them --
let's be honest --

00:03:14.634 --> 00:03:16.567
very funny,
but very dumb questions.

00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:17.901
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.901 --> 00:03:20.367
-Yeah.
Very deep questions sometimes.

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:21.701
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:23.200
One of my favorites
in the series,

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.133
you believe the Renaissance,
Cunk believes was --

00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:30.734
thought was a sauce.
-A nice sauce, yeah.

00:03:30.734 --> 00:03:32.734
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.734 --> 00:03:35.434
-Now, while you're speaking
to this Renaissance expert,

00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:36.901
how much do they know
ahead of time,

00:03:36.901 --> 00:03:37.934
when you shoot those,

00:03:37.934 --> 00:03:39.467
what they're
about to embark upon?

00:03:39.467 --> 00:03:43.100
-They don't know -- Well, they
know that it's a comedy now.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.968
The cat is out of the bag now.
-Right.

00:03:44.968 --> 00:03:46.767
-But they play along.
But they don't know

00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:48.133
what I'm going to ask.
-Yep.

00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:50.467
-So they don't know
any of the questions.

00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:53.467
And it's nice to sort of
back people into a corner...

00:03:53.467 --> 00:03:55.000
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.968
-...and watch them struggle.
-They do a very --

00:03:57.968 --> 00:04:02.400
They do a very nice job
of not breaking, because --

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.067
-They break.
-Yeah, and then you --

00:04:04.067 --> 00:04:05.901
-They just cut it out.
-You just edit it out, yeah.

00:04:05.901 --> 00:04:07.968
[ Laughter ]
Now, do you like them?

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:10.334
So, you, obviously, know
it's unusable once they break.

00:04:10.334 --> 00:04:12.434
But do you, as a performer,
enjoy the moment

00:04:12.434 --> 00:04:14.734
that you make these professors
and art historians laugh?

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:17.901
-Yeah, but you think "Oh, no,
we'll have to go back

00:04:17.901 --> 00:04:20.067
and do it again."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:23.133
-You do a wonderful job.

00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:26.000
It feels as though, you are --
everybody who makes this show

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.734
is experts in the sort of tropes

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:30.934
of these historical reenactment
or travel shows.

00:04:30.934 --> 00:04:33.868
You do a great amount
of walking in the woods.

00:04:33.868 --> 00:04:35.467
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:35.467 --> 00:04:38.634
-A lot of walking and talking.
And we were talking backstage,

00:04:38.634 --> 00:04:40.868
there's a scene
in the second episode

00:04:40.868 --> 00:04:43.434
where, I mean, I think it's
about a four-minute monolog

00:04:43.434 --> 00:04:45.033
where you're walking
around a castle.

00:04:45.033 --> 00:04:46.534
-Yeah.
-I'm so impressed

00:04:46.534 --> 00:04:47.968
by the amount of
walking and talking you do.

00:04:47.968 --> 00:04:49.334
-I do a lot of walking.
-Yeah.

00:04:49.334 --> 00:04:50.534
Do you enjoy being
out in the woods,

00:04:50.534 --> 00:04:53.000
doing your walking and talking?
-Uh, not really.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.367
It's very cold.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.367 --> 00:04:56.901
-But it's just
one of those tropes

00:04:56.901 --> 00:04:59.033
that history documentaries
always have.

00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:00.834
They're always walking
through the woods.

00:05:00.834 --> 00:05:02.200
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.767
Or just staring off
into the middle distance.

00:05:04.767 --> 00:05:06.501
[ Laughter ]
You know?

00:05:06.501 --> 00:05:09.834
-You do wonderful
as a performer.

00:05:09.834 --> 00:05:12.801
I'm very taken
with how Cunk trips a lot.

00:05:12.801 --> 00:05:16.601
-I love a trip.
I love a bit of physical comedy.

00:05:16.601 --> 00:05:22.100
-They're all very little trips
that you try to then play off.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.267
-Yeah.

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:24.868
-I'm such a fan
of the little trip.

00:05:24.868 --> 00:05:27.367
-Are you? I love a trip!
-Oh, I love a trip.

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:31.200
Especially from someone
who is hosting a history show

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:32.834
and there's
this real gravitas to it,

00:05:32.834 --> 00:05:34.667
and then a little trip.
-Fall over. Yeah.

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.100
-Yeah.
-Do you know

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.534
when you've done a good one
when you're shooting?

00:05:37.534 --> 00:05:39.300
You're like, "Perfect, yeah"?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.834
I mean, there was one I rolled
down a hill at one point.

00:05:41.834 --> 00:05:43.334
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.334 --> 00:05:45.701
Which I was at the top
of a sand dune.

00:05:45.701 --> 00:05:48.367
And I thought -- I didn't tell
anyone I was going to do it.

00:05:48.367 --> 00:05:50.801
I just thought, "This is
going to be quite boring,

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:54.200
me walking down a sand dune.
So, I'm just gonna fall over."

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.534
[ Laughter ]

00:05:55.534 --> 00:05:58.100
No one came to help me.
I was rolling.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.534
[ Laughter ]
Just kept rolling.

00:06:00.534 --> 00:06:02.467
And they were just filming.

00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:04.667
[ Laughter ]

00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:06.767
-This is how much you care
about your craft,

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:08.634
you're willing to take a header.
-I'm just worried

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:11.434
that people are gonna be bored,
so I think, "I'll fall over."

00:06:11.434 --> 00:06:13.100
[ Laughter ]
-Now, again,

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.300
because there's
such attention to detail,

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.767
have people, when this
first started in the U.K.,

00:06:17.767 --> 00:06:20.133
did people think
it was a real show?

00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:21.467
-Yes.
-Okay, so, they did.

00:06:21.467 --> 00:06:23.067
-And they still do.
-They still do?

00:06:23.067 --> 00:06:25.100
[ Laughter ]
-On Twitter, I still get people

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:27.801
going, "This presenter
is terrible."

00:06:27.801 --> 00:06:30.234
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.234 --> 00:06:32.834
"She doesn't know
what she's talking about.

00:06:32.834 --> 00:06:34.601
She's getting
all these facts wrong."

00:06:34.601 --> 00:06:36.534
[ Laughter ]

00:06:36.534 --> 00:06:38.767
"The BBC
are really dumbing down."

00:06:38.767 --> 00:06:41.801
[ Laughter ]
-Now, were you --

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:44.200
Did you originally think
comedy was your path?

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.634
Or did you think
just acting in general?

00:06:46.634 --> 00:06:50.634
-No. I wanted to be
a comedy actor from age 15.

00:06:50.634 --> 00:06:52.767
-Oh, wonderful.
-That's what I wanted to do.

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:55.901
You know, I grew up watching,
like, Harold Lloyd

00:06:55.901 --> 00:06:58.801
and Laurel &amp; Hardy, and
I was just, you know,

00:06:58.801 --> 00:07:01.133
obsessed
that's what I wanted to do.

00:07:01.133 --> 00:07:03.067
-And did you do it at all
at university?

00:07:03.067 --> 00:07:04.734
Or did you --
-I went to drama school.

00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:06.000
-Okay, gotcha.
-Yeah.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.000
-And then how were they
with attempting comedy?

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:09.634
Were they alright with it?
-Well, I went --

00:07:09.634 --> 00:07:13.167
I went to the head of the school
and I said,

00:07:13.167 --> 00:07:16.234
"Look, I'm not interested
in Shakespeare, you know?

00:07:16.234 --> 00:07:19.667
I only want to do comedy.
So just let me do --

00:07:19.667 --> 00:07:21.934
I just want to get better
at comedy."

00:07:21.934 --> 00:07:26.567
And he started giving me, like,
Lady Macbeth, parts like that.

00:07:26.567 --> 00:07:30.434
But, luckily, I got big laughs
as lady Macbeth.

00:07:30.434 --> 00:07:31.968
[ Laughter ]
I was really proud.

00:07:31.968 --> 00:07:33.834
-To this day, they say you were
the funniest Lady Macbeth.

00:07:33.834 --> 00:07:35.834
-The funniest Lady Macbeth,
yeah.

00:07:35.834 --> 00:07:38.434
-And then, it is true that you
did stand-up for a while?

00:07:38.434 --> 00:07:40.901
-I did it for nearly 10 years.
-And did you love it?

00:07:40.901 --> 00:07:43.100
-I hated it.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:46.467
I hated it.
Did you do stand-up?

00:07:46.467 --> 00:07:47.734
-I do, still, a little.
-Did you hate --

00:07:47.734 --> 00:07:49.267
You still do it?
-I do, yeah.

00:07:49.267 --> 00:07:51.300
-Do you hate it?
-I don't hate it, no.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.767
-Why not? It's awful.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:53.767 --> 00:07:57.534
-What did you hate about it?
-just going about at night

00:07:57.534 --> 00:08:00.200
on your own,
traveling around late at night.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:04.033
Dealing with crowds
that, you know, were horrible.

00:08:04.033 --> 00:08:05.667
[ Laughter ]
You could just tell,

00:08:05.667 --> 00:08:07.601
some nights,
they were just horrible people.

00:08:07.601 --> 00:08:10.334
And be like, "Ugh."
You'd have to pander to them.

00:08:10.334 --> 00:08:13.000
-Yeah.
-I just-- I just felt

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.300
physically sick
every time I did it.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:16.767
[ Laughter ]
-Do you feel great relief

00:08:16.767 --> 00:08:18.734
being past
that part of your career?

00:08:18.734 --> 00:08:21.901
-Yes, absolutely, I mean,
you know, fingers crossed.

00:08:21.901 --> 00:08:23.767
Knock on wood.
-Well, I certainly --

00:08:23.767 --> 00:08:25.467
-I might have to go back
at some point.

00:08:25.467 --> 00:08:27.367
-Base on what you've said,
I won't be very excited

00:08:27.367 --> 00:08:29.033
if I'm in an audience
and you walk out.

00:08:29.033 --> 00:08:31.067
[ Laughter ]

00:08:31.067 --> 00:08:33.334
-Oh, no.
-And now, we were talking

00:08:33.334 --> 00:08:35.067
about this backstage,
but, you know,

00:08:35.067 --> 00:08:36.968
this hasn't been on Netflix
very long,

00:08:36.968 --> 00:08:38.234
but here you are
in New York City,

00:08:38.234 --> 00:08:40.934
and people are recognizing
you from "Cunk"?

00:08:40.934 --> 00:08:42.868
-It's amazing.
People are lovely.

00:08:42.868 --> 00:08:46.267
They're so enthusiastic.
-Well, it is a show

00:08:46.267 --> 00:08:48.000
that deserves
all the enthusiasm.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:49.434
It's really wonderful.
And thank you so much

00:08:49.434 --> 00:08:51.200
for being here.
-Thank you for having me.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.100
-I'm just delighted
that you're here.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.968
[ Cheers and applause ]
You guys, that's Diane Morgan.

00:08:54.968 --> 00:08:56.801
"Cunk on Earth"
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:08:56.801 --> 00:08:58.467
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

